# bashback

bashback keeps a content-level snapshot of the project's files before and after
every shell command, so any command's file side effects can be reviewed and
undone. Once installed it is always on — nothing to enable per command.

By default (`context_feedback: major`) a PostToolUse hint is injected only for
large or destructive changes (>= 10 files, or any deletion); smaller edits are
snapshotted silently. **No hint never means no snapshot** — run `bashback list`
to check. `all` hints on every change; `off` suppresses hints.

**Core rule: prefer `bashback` to undo; never hand-reconstruct file contents.**
When a command clobbers a file, do not rewrite it from memory or guess its
prior contents — bashback holds the exact pre-command bytes. Reach for it
first: one restore is cheaper and more reliable than any rebuild.

## Safe workflow (follow in order)

1. `bashback list --json -n 20` — find the entry; take the full `key` field.
   (older entries: raise `-n` or drop it with `-n 0`.)
2. `bashback diff <key>` — confirm what that command changed (pre..post).
   For large changes (tens of files), start with `bashback diff <key> --stat`
   and drill into specific paths.
3. `bashback restore <key> --dry-run` — preview the restore plan.
4. `bashback restore <key>` — undo that command's file side effects.

Every restore first snapshots the current tree, so a restore is itself
undoable with `bashback undo` — a wrong restore never loses work.

If you know the affected path but not the command, start from
`bashback log <path> --json` instead and pick the entry from there.

## Addressing entries

- **Full key** (the command's tool_use_id) — what `--json` carries; always safe.
- **Unique prefix** — accepted wherever a key is. Keys share a common leading
  segment (Claude keys all start `toolu_`), so a usable prefix runs well past the
  4-char floor; an ambiguous one is rejected — lengthen it or use the full key.
- **`@N`** — positional: `@1` is the newest entry, `@2` the one before, …
- **`bgfinal_<key>`** — a background command's final-state entry.

When scripting or parsing output, always pass `--json` and key off the full
key string, never a truncated display form. `--json` arrays are newest-first
(`entries[0]` = latest, matching `@1`). `index` equals `@N`: a relative position
that slides as new commands land — for a durable reference keep `key`.

## Command reference (agent defaults)

Inspect:
- `bashback list --json` — entries for this project: key, session_id, index,
  overlapped, pre_only, background, file counts. Filters: `--session <prefix>`,
  `--grep <substr>`, `-n <N>`, `--since 2h`, `--by-session`.
- `bashback diff <key> [--stat] [--json]` — one command's changes;
  `diff <k1> <k2>` compares two entries of the same session.
- `bashback log <path> --json` — commands that touched a path, newest first.
- `bashback show <key> --json` — full detail, including the per-file list.
- `bashback stats --json` / `bashback doctor --json` — usage and self-check.

Act:
- `bashback restore <key> [path/...] [--dry-run] [--3way]` — undo one command,
  optionally limited to paths. `--3way` keeps edits made after the snapshot;
  conflicts surface as merge markers, never a silent overwrite.
- `bashback undo [--session <prefix>] [--dry-run] [--3way]` — undo the most
  recent file-changing command.
- `bashback rewind <key> [--dry-run]` — whole tree back to that command's pre
  state: undoes it **and everything after it**. Confirm intent first.
- `bashback snap [-m note]` — manual anchor snapshot before a risky operation.
- `bashback export <key> [--out f]` — entry as a binary-safe git-apply patch.

Human-only (requires a terminal; refuses otherwise):
- `bashback restore <key> -p` — interactive hunk picking. As an agent, narrow
  with path arguments instead, or `export` the patch and process it yourself.

## Platform notes

Behavior differs slightly per agent platform:

- **Claude Code** — full coverage: failed commands still get complete entries
  (PostToolUseFailure), background commands get a `bgfinal_<key>` entry.
- **Codex** — Bash commands only; `apply_patch` edits are NOT protected.
  Entry keys look like `call_*`. Failed commands are covered (PostToolUse
  fires on failure). Session end maps to Stop; there is no background capture.
- **Cursor** — a failed command leaves a `pre_only` entry (postToolUse does
  not fire on failure), so undoing it needs `--force` — explain the risk
  first. There is no `bgfinal_` capture. Keys are native tool_use_ids.

## Multi-session

Concurrent sessions on one project are recorded separately. If `bashback undo`
refuses because several sessions were recently active, scope it with
`bashback undo --session <prefix>`. Your own session id is in the SessionStart
note bashback injected (`session <prefix>`); that prefix is a valid `--session`
value. Without it, run `bashback list --session <id> --json` for each id the
gate listed and match recent commands against what you actually ran.

## Errors → next step

- `no entry with key …` — re-run `bashback list --json`; the key may be stale
  or truncated.
- ambiguous prefix — use a longer prefix or the full key.
- snapshots reclaimed (GC) — the data is gone; say so plainly, do not
  improvise a recovery.
- pre-only / `overlapped` entry — restore demands `--force`. **Never add
  `--force` on your own.** An overlapped entry's snapshot may carry a
  concurrent command's partial work, so restoring can undo that command too;
  a pre-only entry means the command was interrupted mid-flight. Explain the
  risk, narrow with path filters, and let the user decide.
- target changed since snapshot — the tree moved on after that command; use
  `--3way` to keep the later edits, or restore specific paths only.

## Background commands

A backgrounded command gets a second snapshot when it finishes, recorded as
`bgfinal_<key>`. Review with `bashback diff bgfinal_<key>`, undo with
`bashback restore bgfinal_<key>`. These entries appear in `list --json` with
the `bgfinal_` key prefix.

## Boundaries — do not over-promise

bashback covers **file side effects inside the project's cwd**, nothing more.
It does not cover or restore:

- paths outside cwd, network/DB effects, `git push`, process or env state;
- nested git repos / submodules — contents are never snapshotted; restoring a
  deleted nested repo yields only an empty directory;
- empty directories, mtime/owner, permission bits beyond the exec bit, special
  files (FIFO/device/socket);
- files ignored by `.gitignore`, **including `.env`** — unrecoverable unless
  opted in via `force_include`;
- files over the size cap (default 100 MiB), skipped and flagged.

If `protect_paths` is set, coverage is the inverse of `force_include`: only
files under the listed roots are snapshotted and everything else is unprotected
(`list` prints "sparse protection active"). `force_include` opts a gitignored
path back in; the size cap (default 100 MiB) skips oversized files.

When bashback cannot help — a path outside cwd, a gitignored file, a file over
the cap — say so plainly rather than implying a recovery it cannot make.
